# =============================================================================
#  creds.ps1  -  the network credentials this PC has saved, and how to fix them
# =============================================================================
#
#  AUTHOR
#  ------
#  KD  -  Computer Consultancy
#  kudadonbe@outlook.com
#
#  WHAT IT DOES
#  ------------
#  Shows what is in this PC's Credential Manager, adds an entry for a host,
#  and deletes the ones that have gone stale. Nothing more - but it is the
#  thing half the "the network is broken" calls actually come down to.
#
#  WHY IT IS ITS OWN TOOL
#  ----------------------
#  Every tool that reaches another PC - connecting a printer, reading a PC's
#  shares, staging a driver, pulling an inventory - authenticates through the
#  same stored credentials, and all of them fail the same way when an entry
#  goes stale. It was reachable only from inside 'printer', which is the wrong
#  door when the thing that is broken is a file share.
#
#  THE FAILURE WORTH KNOWING
#  -------------------------
#  A REFUSED query returns an EMPTY list, not an error. Windows then reports
#  that the host shares nothing - which reads like a firewall or a Windows
#  update problem, and is neither. After a domain password change every stored
#  entry goes stale at once, so shares and printers appear to vanish across the
#  office; that usually lands near a patch Tuesday and takes the blame for it.
#  Uninstalling the update seems to fix it because of the reboot, so the wrong
#  cure sticks and the real one is never found.
#
#  Two traps this flags in the list:
#    - a user stored with NO domain prefix ('jsmith' rather than 'DOM\jsmith'),
#      which a domain host rejects outright as "no such user" (0xc0000064),
#    - a TERMSRV/ entry, which is Remote Desktop ONLY and does nothing for file
#      or print access - easy to mistake for proof that the host is covered.
#
#  HOW TO RUN
#  ----------
#  From the pen or share menu (kd), pick "creds". Or directly:
#     irm <host>/creds | iex
#
#  It is INTERACTIVE - it asks, it does not take parameters. (Parameters
#  cannot be passed when a tool is run through iex, which is how the menu and
#  the share launch every tool, so a menu tool has to prompt.)
#
#  SAFETY
#  ------
#  Listing changes nothing. Adding and deleting change Credential Manager for
#  the CURRENT USER only, and deleting always confirms first. No elevation is
#  needed, and that is deliberate - see the note below.
# =============================================================================

